## Goal
A sample PCA from the gene counts, shown as one plot coloured by treated versus untreated.
It shows how the six samples (A1-A3 untreated, B1-B3 treated) sit relative to one another on
the main axes of variation in expression.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| `counts_gene` (native) | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper` | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv` |
| `design` (native) | `01_prepare_samplesheets` | `01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv` |

## Method
All steps run in one Python script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`, in the `gars-bio` conda
environment (pandas, numpy, scikit-learn, matplotlib), submitted as a single batch job. Inputs
are only read, never modified.

1. **Resolve inputs.** Call `_system/resolve_artifact.py --project projects/run-9f2f09f5
   --assay rnaseq_bulk --consumes counts_gene design` and read the two paths it returns.
   If resolution fails, stop.
2. **Load.** Read the counts TSV (`gene_id`, `gene_name`, then one column per sample); index
   by `gene_id` and drop `gene_name`. Read the design CSV. Check that the count columns and the
   design's `sample_id` values are the same set of six samples; stop with an error if not.
   Order samples as in the design table.
3. **Normalise.** Counts are Salmon length-scaled estimates (non-integer). Convert each sample
   to counts per million (divide by the sample's column total, multiply by 1e6), then take
   `log2(CPM + 1)`.
4. **Filter.** Keep genes with CPM >= 1 in at least 3 samples (the size of the smallest
   condition group), then drop any remaining gene with zero variance across samples. Record the
   number of genes kept.
5. **PCA.** Samples as rows, genes as columns. Mean-centre each gene (no unit-variance
   scaling). `sklearn.decomposition.PCA(n_components=min(n_samples, n_genes))` with the default
   full SVD; no random component. Record the explained-variance ratio of each PC.
6. **Plot.** One scatter of PC1 (x) against PC2 (y), one point per sample, coloured by the
   design's `condition` column (two colours: `treated`, `untreated`), each point labelled with
   its `sample_id`, legend titled `condition`. Axis labels carry the percent variance explained
   (e.g. `PC1 (xx.x%)`). Title states the gene count used and the transform
   (`log2(CPM+1)`). Saved with matplotlib at 300 dpi.
7. **Tables.** Write each sample's coordinates on every PC, with its `condition`, to the scores
   table; write each PC's explained-variance ratio, and the number of genes used, to a separate
   variance table (see Outputs).

## Outputs
Types come from the closed vocabulary in _references/artifact_types.md.

| File | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `results/sample_pca_pc1_pc2.png` | `figure` | PC1 vs PC2 scatter, one point per sample, coloured treated vs untreated, axes labelled with % variance explained |
| `results/sample_pca_scores.tsv` | `table` | one row per sample: `sample_id`, `condition`, `PC1` ... `PCn` coordinates |
| `results/sample_pca_variance.tsv` | `table` | one row per PC: `pc`, `explained_variance_ratio`, `n_genes` (the filtered gene count, same on every row) |

## Execution
Runs: batch

One batch job through `_system/executorlib.py submit`, in `gars-bio`. The matrix is 200 genes
x 6 samples: expected wall time under one minute, memory well under 1 GB, outputs under 1 MB
in total.
